Clivus, -i, m., slope, ascent, gently sloping hill. If you look up this word in a methodical vocabulary, such as the one we indicate in the BIBLIOGRAPHICAL INDEX, it will take you a little longer than in the dictionary, but also a little more instructive. You will find there the interesting derivatives, for example:
— proclivis, which slopes, inclined; whence inclined or easy to do and proclivitas, slope;
— declivis, which descends in a slope, and declivitas, descent;
— acclivis, which climbs in a slope, and acclivitas, ascent;
— inclinare, to make lean, whence inclinatio, inclination or inclination;
— declinare, to turn aside, to deflect, to decline (a noun, to conjugate the verb, or still to be in decline).
By using this method you will learn more and retain better. But do not therefore reject the use of the dictionary: it is indispensable for VERIFYING what you believe you know.
